{"number": 47, "text": "                      Exercises\n\n\n              20.1    Look      Correct the collocation errors in these sentences.\n                         It can be hard to carry out a serious conversation in a noisy room.\n\n                         He finds it very difficult to open his head and talk about his feelings to anyone.\n                         | don’t like discussing things with people who always want to gain every argument.\n                         My mother always used to tell me not to spill unkind rumours.\n                         | usually find it better not to get driven into an argument with Paul.\n                        We  had a very enjoyable time just sitting in the park enjoying some idling chatter.\n                         | managed to resist all his attempts to engage me into conversation.\n                         I’ve never talked to him much - we’ve done no more than change pleasantries.\n             20.2     Complete  each sentence  using a word from the box in the appropriate form.\n\n                       bring     broach     drop     hold     lose    strong    take     tough\n\n                      1 t...                 the argument  because | didn’t know enough about the subject.\n                      2  think it’s time We                   the subject of our marriage with our parents.\n                      3 Noone   Will                   your ideas seriously unless you present them more effectively.\n                      4 My  mother can’t get used to hearing well-dressed young people USING\n                        language  in public places.\n                      5 Whenever  | try to                   up the subject of moving to London, he leaves the room.\n                      6 I’ve said I’m sorry -— why Can’t YOU                    the subject?\n                      T They’re Sure to ASk YOU SOME                    questions at your interview but you can take\n                        time to think before you answer.\n                      8 | felt so ill last week that | could barely              a conversation.\n                      Match the two  parts of these collocations.\n                      1 juicy                promise\n                      2 broad                generalisations\n                      3 exchange             gambit\n                      4 take                 question\n                      5 four-letter          seriously\n                      6 tough                word\n                      7 rash                 gossip\n                      8 opening              news\n             20.4     Rewrite each sentence  using the word in brackets.\n                       1  Please stop talking about this subject immediately. (DROP)\n                          Sam has a habit of promising things that he has no intention of doing.\n\n                          You must give due importance to the lab’s safety regulations. (SERIOUSLY)\n                          It’s better not to spend too long trying to make your point. (OVERSTATE)\n                          Could we please start talking about something else? (SUBJECT)\n                          Did you hear the rumours that people were spreading about your boss last\n                          year? (FLYING)\n                          There are rather a lot of rude words in the play. (LANGUAGE)\n                       ON\n                          Such general statements tend to be rather meaningless. (BROAD)\n                          The way he began the conversation took me by surprise. (GAMBIT)\n                          The children asked me lots and lots of questions about my trip. (BOMBARD)\n\n\n                                                                         English Collocations in Use Advanced", "char_count": 3748, "mean_conf": 89.45, "word_count": 440, "words": [{"text": "Exercises", "x0": 453, "y0": 223, "x1": 798, "y1": 288, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "